# The Wednesday Morning at the Gift Shop

Ally Whittier unlocked the shop door at eight-fifty-two, turned the small brass lamp on the counter, and set the coffee to brew in the little Mr. Coffee the Auxiliary bought at the Ames on Route 15 in the November of 1993.

Alice Marie "Ally" Cutter Whittier — Morristown Graded '58, Peoples Academy '61, Middlebury '65, a third-grade teacher at Peoples Academy from the September of 1965 until the June of 2003, married to Warren Elliott Whittier at the small white Congregational church on Bridge Street on a warm Saturday in the July of 1968, widowed on the tenth of October, 2019 — had driven the four miles from home that morning in the blue 2014 Subaru Forester with 128,000 miles on it, parked in the Auxiliary spot behind the ambulance bay, and walked the small back hallway past the linen closet, the way she has walked it, every Wednesday morning at eight-fifty, since a Wednesday morning in the April of 1994.

Warren had died at home, in the small back bedroom of the farmhouse at 217 Stagecoach Road, on a Thursday morning at four past six, of the Parkinson's disease Dr. Rebecca Fulton at Copley Neurology had diagnosed in the March of 2013. Diane Beliveau, RN, from Copley Hospice, had been in the room. Ally had been in the room. The old golden retriever Barnaby had been on the braided rug.

The Auxiliary Gift Shop, off the main corridor on the ground floor, one door down from the emergency-room waiting area, had opened on the eleventh of November, 1972, in a small alcove that had been, until the September before, a supply closet for Central Sterile. Mrs. Katherine Foss Peabody, the Auxiliary president — Copley '39, wife of Dr. Kenneth Peabody, chief of medicine from the June of 1946 until the June of 1974 — had asked the maintenance crew to hang two pine shelves, a small brass bell over the door, and a hand-lettered sign that read, in the plain painted letters her granddaughter Sally, then a junior at Peoples Academy, had lettered on a pine board: *The Auxiliary Gift Shop. Open Tuesday through Friday, nine to noon. All proceeds to the hospital.*

Every Wednesday morning shift since has been kept in the green cloth-bound quarto in the drawer below the register.

## What the Book is

The Wednesday log is a plain green cloth-bound quarto, eight and a half by eleven, three hundred and sixty pages, from the small Whitman-Norwood printing house on Elm Street in Waterbury that has bound Vermont town-clerk record books since 1893. Mrs. Peabody bought the first one, with her own money, at Bear Pond Books in Montpelier on the second Wednesday of November, 1972. She wrote, on the top of page one, in her own careful copperplate:

*A page a Wednesday. Who came in. What they bought. What they said. Any small thing you would want the next Wednesday's girl to know.*

The current book — Ally's book, volume XIV — was started on the second Wednesday of March, 2019, seven months before Warren died. It is two hundred and eleven pages full. Ally has kept every page since the tenth of April, 2019.

The page for this morning, the second-to-last written page in the book, reads, in Ally's round Papermate hand, at the top left corner where the date belongs:

*Wednesday, 22 July 2026. Sunny. Overcast by eleven.*

Below it, in the small numbered list she has kept the way she used to keep the list of who had turned in a book report on a Tuesday morning at Peoples Academy in the March of 1978:

*1. Mrs. Rita Cochran, 9:07. In for a Get-Well card for her sister-in-law Barbara Cochran Lee, who fell on the porch steps on Elmore Mountain Road on Sunday and is on Two North with a fractured hip. Bought the Peggy Karr fused-glass hummingbird card, the white envelope, and the small tin of Cabot butter mints. Barbara is the widow of the late Dr. Peter Lee, orthopedics, retired 2011. Their daughter Anna is in the second grade at Elmore School.*

*2. Judge Timothy McSweeney, 9:41. In for the anniversary card for his wife Ellen (fifty years on the Saturday). Bought the small watercolor of a sugar maple by the Craftsbury artist Susan Bull Riley. Told me, quietly, at the register, that his brother David (Middlebury '69, retired VP at John Hancock in Boston) has just been moved into memory care at the Manor in Morrisville. Ellen has cried every morning this week.*

*3. Sally Peabody Wing, 10:14. In to drop off the small stack of new hand-knit hats her mother Kate has made for the newborn nursery since May. Sally is now the Auxiliary treasurer. Told me, over coffee at the counter, that the family is going to give the small brown envelope in Kate's rolltop desk to the hospice foundation this fall. Kate is ninety-one on the Tuesday.*

*4. Mrs. Elizabeth Chandler, 11:07. In to buy a plain white sympathy card for the Ashley family — the young mother of two who died on the third floor on Monday of the same ovarian cancer that took Nancy Chandler in 2011. Elizabeth carried the card out slowly and did not speak.*

*5. Note to next week's girl: Barbara Lee on Two North is going to need someone to sit with her Thursday afternoon while Anna is at ballet in Stowe. I can go. I will bring the small blue tin of Bird's Custard she used to buy at the co-op.*

Ally closed the book at eleven-fifty-eight, put it back in the drawer beneath the register, turned off the Mr. Coffee, and locked the door with the same small brass key at twelve past twelve.

The book went back into the drawer.

The drawer stayed shut until the following Wednesday at eight-fifty-two.

## What the second floor did not know

On the second floor of the same brick building, on the same Wednesday morning at nine-oh-four, the director of the Copley Hospital Foundation — a warm and capable woman named Meredith Kimball Doyle, hired in the January of 2024 from the development office at a small liberal-arts college outside Boston — was looking at the third quarterly board report on a laptop at a glass-topped desk under a window that faces the same ambulance bay Ally had parked behind at eight-forty-seven.

The file open on Meredith's second monitor, in the small mail-merge window of the annual-fund appeal that would go to the printer at Villanti & Sons on the second Tuesday of August, read:

*WHITTIER, ALICE M. — 217 Stagecoach Road, Morrisville VT 05661. Lapsed 2004. Last gift $50 (memorial for Warren Cutter Sr., her father, 2004). Spouse deceased 10/2019. No engagement noted. Suggested ask: $25.*

Meredith had never met Ally Whittier.

Meredith had never walked down the small back hallway past the linen closet.

Meredith had never opened the green cloth-bound quarto in the drawer beneath the register.

Nobody in the Foundation office, on the second floor of the small brick building on Washington Highway, had opened it since the summer of 2011.

## What the Book knows

The Wednesday log is the single richest weekly document the Copley Hospital Foundation will ever have access to and does not read.

It is written *by* a donor (a lapsed one, on paper — a lifelong one, in every other measurable way), *about* other donors, *in the plain hand of a woman who has taught the granddaughters of half the board*, at the register of the small pine counter one flight of stairs below the Foundation office. It is unsolicited. It does not use a wealth screen. It does not run through Wealth Engine or DonorSearch or iWave. It is the primary source, in the small unhurried voice of a third-grade teacher, on a Wednesday morning at eleven-fifty-eight, in blue Papermate ink.

It says, on a single page:

The hip on Two North, and the daughter in ballet in Stowe.

The fiftieth anniversary on Saturday, and the brother in memory care at the Manor.

The rolltop desk, the small brown envelope, the ninety-first birthday on Tuesday, and the hospice foundation this fall.

The young mother of two on the third floor, and the woman who carried the sympathy card out slowly and did not speak.

The volunteer, at the counter, who will drive to Two North on Thursday afternoon with a small blue tin of Bird's Custard because she has been paying attention since the fall of 1950.

If a screening vendor produced any one of those paragraphs on a Tuesday afternoon in July and emailed it to Meredith Doyle, she would clear her calendar Wednesday and walk down one flight of stairs on Thursday.

The paragraph was in the drawer at eleven-fifty-eight on Wednesday morning.

The drawer is fifteen feet below her desk.

## A small honest note about Rōmy

Rōmy did not write the Wednesday log. Ally did. Ally wrote it at the small pine counter, at the end of every Wednesday morning shift, in the blue Papermate ink she used to write report-card comments in from 1965 until 2003, on the page whose date she pencilled in at eight-fifty-two before she brewed the coffee.

What Rōmy can do is read the log.

Not instead of Meredith Doyle. Meredith Doyle is the reason the log matters at all. She is the one who will read Wednesday's page on the walk down the stairs on Thursday morning, and know, before she pushes open the door of Two North to sit for ten minutes with Barbara Cochran Lee, that Barbara is the widow of Peter Lee, orthopedics, retired 2011, that Anna is in the second grade at Elmore School, that Bird's Custard is the right small tin to bring, and that the woman at the register who wrote the paragraph is the same Ally Whittier her mail-merge field says has been *lapsed since 2004.*

Rōmy is the quiet Thursday-morning list on Meredith's desk that says: *these are the four names Ally wrote down on Wednesday. Here is what she wrote. Here is who Ally is. The stairs are at the end of the hall.*

The reading is Meredith's. The list is the tool's. The paragraph is Ally's.

## A small assignment, with love ♡

This week, or on the first Wednesday morning in August, before the fall appeal goes to the printer at Villanti & Sons, walk down the stairs.

Find the gift shop. Find the register. Find the volunteer at the counter whose name has been on the Auxiliary schedule since before your director of development graduated from college.

Do not ask her for anything.

Sit down at the small round table by the poinsettia the Rotary sent at Christmas. Ask her, quietly, how Barbara Lee's hip is doing. Ask her how Ellen McSweeney is holding up. Ask her whether Kate Peabody is having a good week.

Then ask her, before you leave, if she keeps a log.

She does.

She has kept a log every Wednesday since a Wednesday morning in the April of 1994.

Read a page.

Read it in the plain unhurried way Ally Whittier writes it at eleven-fifty-eight on Wednesday morning, at the small pine counter, in the blue Papermate ink, on the page whose date she pencilled in at eight-fifty-two before she brewed the coffee.

When you find the name of a donor you know — a former Board of Overseers member, a lapsed sustaining member from 2004, the widow of the chief of medicine from 1974 — do not, this week, put a case for support in the envelope.

Take a plain notecard from the top drawer of your credenza. Say the donor's name once. Say the small specific thing the log printed on the page — the fiftieth anniversary, the granddaughter at Elmore, the small blue tin of Bird's Custard — and say, in the plain language of a person who was paying attention, that Ally wrote it down on Wednesday morning at the counter.

Walk it, on your lunch break, down the small back hallway past the linen closet, to the pine counter by the emergency-room waiting area.

Hand it to Ally.

Ask her to give it to Barbara on Thursday afternoon, when she drives to Two North with the small blue tin of Bird's Custard from the co-op.

Ally wrote the page at eleven-fifty-eight on Wednesday morning at the small pine counter.

The drawer is fifteen feet below your desk.

Please walk down the stairs. ♡
